%T A400281 26388,36532,82570,114307,258360,357676,808401,1119152,2529451,
%U A400281 3501794,7914524,10956932,24764120,34283682,77485565,107271744,242447991,335647335,758606096,1050221595
%N A400281 Number of self-conjugate skew partitions of n whose diagrams have no empty rows and columns.
%C A400281 A skew partition (lambda, mu) is self-conjugate if (lambda, mu) = (lambda', mu'), where the prime denotes the conjugate (transpose) of a partition. Equivalently, its skew Young diagram is symmetric with respect to the main diagonal.
%C A400281 A skew partition is called reduced if its skew Young diagram has no empty rows or columns. A225114(n) is the number of reduced skew partitions of n, while a(n) is the number of self-conjugate reduced skew partitions of n. For a self-conjugate skew partition the condition 'has no empty rows' is equivalent to 'has no empty columns' by symmetry.
%C A400281 Conjugation is an involution on reduced skew partitions of n, and its fixed points are the self-conjugate ones.
%C A400281 The difference A225114(n) - a(n) is always even. Therefore, (A225114(n) - a(n)) / 2 is the number of unordered pairs {S, S'} of distinct mutually conjugate skew partitions counted by A225114(n).
%C A400281 The case mu = () gives the ordinary self-conjugate partitions of n, counted by A000700. Consequently, a(n) >= A000700(n), and a(n) - A000700(n) is the number of self-conjugate skew partitions of n with nonempty inner partition.
%D A400281 R. P. Stanley, Enumerative combinatorics. Vol. 2. Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics 62 (1999), Cambridge University Press.
%H A400281 Peter Luschny, <a href="/A400281/b400281.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..100</a>.
%H A400281 F. Bergeron, <a href="https://bergeron.math.uqam.ca/wp-content/uploads/2019/09/Symmetric-Functions.pdf">Symmetric Functions and Rectangular Catalan Combinatorics</a>, 2019.
%H A400281 Sara C. Billey, Matjaž Konvalinka, and Joshua P. Swanson, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aam.2019.101972">Asymptotic normality of the major index on standard tableaux</a>, Advances in Applied Mathematics, Volume 113, 2020. (p.17)
%H A400281 Philippe Flajolet and Robert Sedgewick, <a href="http://algo.inria.fr/flajolet/Publications/books.html">Analytic Combinatorics</a>, 2009; Chapter IX, §IX.6, Eq. (43), page 661.
%H A400281 Mélodie Lapointe and Pauline Hubert, <a href="https://more-sagemath-tutorials.readthedocs.io/en/latest/tutorial-integer-partitions.html">Integer Partitions</a>, Sage Thematic Tutorial.
%H A400281 Peter Luschny, <a href="/A400281/a400281_1.txt">An encoding for self-conjugate reduced skew partitions</a>.
%H A400281 Bruce E. Sagan, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0304-3975(93)90319-O">Combinatorial proofs of hook generating functions for skew plane partitions</a>, Theoretical Computer Science 117, 273-287, 1993.
%F A400281 A225114(n) - a(n) == 0 (mod 2).
%F A400281 a(n) ~ c * d^n, where d = 1.7688819264526635589787081406813141289243647350827677174778338392... and c = 0.4061859686371348671790167288084445037090884510964960324306727473... if n is even and c = 0.5189901621366767258843300021530527687758758945707133871900539351... if n is odd. - _Vaclav Kotesovec_, Sep 28 2026 (d is A400283. - _Peter Luschny_, Sep 29 2026)
%e A400281 For a(4) = 4 the self-conjugate skew partitions and their Young diagram are (see also the example section in A225114):
%e A400281 [[2, 2], []]
%e A400281   * *
%e A400281   * *
%e A400281 [[3, 1, 1], [1]]
%e A400281   . * *
%e A400281   *
%e A400281   *
%e A400281 [[3, 3, 2], [2, 2]]
%e A400281   . . *
%e A400281   . . *
%e A400281   * *
%e A400281 [[4, 3, 2, 1], [3, 2, 1]]
%e A400281   . . . *
%e A400281   . . *
%e A400281   . *
%e A400281   *
%e A400281 Since only two distinct symbols are needed to represent the Young diagram of a self-conjugate skew partition, more compact formats are possible, such as lists of words over a two-letter alphabet. The Binary Triangular Word (BTW) encoding for the nine self-conjugate skew partitions when n = 5 is shown below. The definition of this encoding is available in the links section.
%e A400281   -------------------------------------------------
%e A400281    BTWord encoding |  (lambda) / (mu)
%e A400281   -------------------------------------------------
%e A400281     0-0-0          | (3, 1, 1) / ()
%e A400281     1-00-0         | (3, 2, 1) / (1)
%e A400281     1-10-00        | (3, 3, 2) / (2, 1)
%e A400281     1-11-000       | (3, 3, 3) / (2, 2)
%e A400281     1-10-0-0       | (4, 2, 1, 1) / (2, 1)
%e A400281     1-10-10-0      | (4, 3, 2, 1) / (3, 1, 1)
%e A400281     1-11-100-0     | (4, 3, 3, 1) / (3, 2, 1)
%e A400281     1-11-110-00    | (4, 4, 3, 2) / (3, 3, 2)
%e A400281     1-11-110-10-0  | (5, 4, 3, 2, 1) / (4, 3, 2, 1)
%o A400281 (SageMath)
%o A400281 # The function 'SkewPartitions' already imposes the reduced condition!
%o A400281 def A400281(n: int): return sum(sp == sp.conjugate() for sp in SkewPartitions(n))
%o A400281 print([A400281(n) for n in range(12)])
%Y A400281 Cf. A225114, A000700, A399982, A400283.
%K A400281 nonn,new
